for the latest on the medical aspects of  the coronavirus crisis we're joined now  by a former Surgeon General of the  United States doctor the vague mortise  served during the last three years of  the Obama administration he's the author  of a new book with a timely topic  together the healing power of human  connection in a sometimes lonely world  that came out just last month dr. Marty  welcome thank you so much it's good to  be with you we are having some success  in reducing the amount of cases in death  states are reopening and loosening  restrictions but treatment options are  still limited and we don't know when a  vaccine will be here so what does the  next few months look like are we on the  right path well I think we have made  progress over the last several months  and we've made progress in a few friends  one is because people have made these  extraordinary sacrifices to stay home to  reduce their activity in their contact  with others we have been able to reduce  what it would have been a much much  worse peak of infections and deaths but  with that said we have still lost nearly  100,000 people to this virus and we have  well over 1.6 million cases and that is  actually still rising so we have to take  that into account and we still have to  be cautious  the second area where we made progress  is on vaccine development while we still  don't have a vaccine and likely won't  have one until 2021 we are seeing more  trials coming out with some early stage  promising results but any path to  reopening safely runs through testing  and this is the one place where if we  have to be honest with ourself we're so  struggling as a country because we still  don't have enough high quality tests  that are available to everyone who needs  them and that is posing a problem for  workplaces that want to open up and for  states that want to track the infection  closely dr. Marty it's Dana Jacobson now  you mentioned workplaces that want to  open and one of the workplaces so many  want to see open is in the sports world  I know you've consulted with the NBA and  formally and deal with some other sports  organizations when it comes to team  sports forget fans just about getting  back  the field or to the field of play how  realistic is it that we will see that in  the coming months well I think it is  possible for us to see sports history  started without fans because we won't  like they won't be able to see crowds  gather in significant numbers until we  have widespread immunity in the  population which essentially means until  we have a vaccine but with that said  there are plans and many leagues are  making to bring back the players so that  they can you start games and theirs can  at least be televised but to do that  requires a few things number one it  requires being able to test with great  frequency and what I hear from many of  the league's and from teams is that they  are one struggling to figure out how  they can get the volume of tests  required to test with that frequency  they're also worried about what it looks  like if a team is testing its players so  frequently when the people in the  community around it are still having  struck out of trouble on getting testing  so that is one big challenge the other  big challenge is how do you reduce your  risk of players getting infected in the  first place because we know that there  is still coronavirus in our communities  if players are going out into the  community coming back interacting with  the team and if one person gets infected  then you're at risk of having to shut  down the entire team so some leagues are  looking at whether they should  quarantine the players for the duration  of play that has implications for their  families and for their own mental health  so these are complicated decisions there  it's it's surmountable and i think we  will see sports over the next several  months but it certainly won't be easy  and again testing is going to be a  critical factor in terms of availability  obviously testing seems to be the key to  everything out there I want to turn to  your book now it deals with how vital  the human connection is and right now in  this time where even though places are  opening up we've had so much social  distancing what is your message to  people out there who still right now may  be feeling very lonely well first I just  want to say that loneliness was a great  challenge that we face as a country long  before the koban 19 pandemic and it is  normal to experience loneliness  throughout our lives it's a signal that  our body sends us that we're lacking  something that we need for a survival  which is human connection  what I found when I research in general  though is a loneliness was far more  common than I ever would have imagined  we have over a fifth over twenty percent  of adults in the United States struggle  with loneliness and many of our children  also struggle with loneliness this  affects how we perform in the workplace  in at school but it turns out it also  has profound impacts on our health with  loneliness increasing our risk for heart  disease for premature death for a  depression and anxiety but here's the  key is that loneliness is something that  we can address we can strengthen human  connection in our life and the reason I  wrote this book is because I wanted  everyone out there who was struggling  with loneliness to understand that you  are not broken and that you are not  alone that many people including myself  have struggled with loneliness over the  years but I also want us to recognize  that social connection is an  extraordinary resource that we have that  if we cultivate it it can be a  performance enhancer it can improve our  health and it can increase our  fulfillment and there's simple ways that  we can strengthen connection even in the  time of kovat nineteen when we're asked  to physically distance we can put 15  minutes aside to spend with the people  we love each day we can make sure the  quality at that time really counts by  eliminating distraction around us we can  look for ways to serve others  recognizing that small acts of service  are powerful ways of addressing  loneliness and counter-intuitively we  can also protect dr. Murray I'm sorry to  cut you off we are out of time it is it  is all good advice and obviously the  book is out there for people to find as  well we wish we had a little bit more  time for you but we do appreciate the  insight thank you
 

